// Vitest Snapshot v1, https://vitest.dev/guide/snapshot.html

exports[`command help snapshots:  1`] = `
"OpenCove CLI — Control projects, spaces, canvas nodes and worker runtimes

Usage: opencove <command> [options]

Workspace:
  project  List projects and find their IDs
  space    List and inspect project spaces
  node     Create, read, update and delete canvas nodes
  canvas   Focus nodes and spaces on the canvas
  agent    List, inspect, prompt, wait for and read agents
  events   Stream agent observations and resume from a saved cursor

Introspection:
  skill    Print the bundled agent usage guide
  whoami   Show your caller identity and permission scope
  context  Orient yourself in a project and space
  api      Discover operations, inspect input schemas and invoke the Control Surface

Runtime:
  ping      Check whether the Control Surface responds
  status    Show protocol, app version and instance identity
  worker    Start, inspect and stop local workers
  runtime   Inspect, verify and prepare managed runtimes
  endpoint  Register and manage remote worker connections
  mount     Connect project folders to worker endpoints
  fs        Read, write and inspect files through the Control Surface
  worktree  List, create and archive Git worktrees
  session   Run agents and inspect or stop their sessions
  pty       Start and attach to terminal sessions

Plugins:
  plugin  Discover, install and manage plugins

Selectors:
  Node, space and project options accept @self, an exact ID, an exact name (case-sensitive), or a unique ID prefix of 6+ characters.
  @self uses your terminal identity. Names are scoped by --project or your own project inside OpenCove; explicit --space constrains every node selector, including full IDs. Ambiguous targets require a full ID.

Global options:
  --endpoint <url>         Connect to this Control Surface URL
  --token <token>          Authenticate with this bearer token
  --user-data <dir>        Use this profile directory
  --timeout <ms>           Set the request timeout in milliseconds (default: 20000)
  --pretty                 Indent JSON output for readability
  --limit <n>              Limit output lists to n items (0 means all); cut object lists add truncated totals, bare arrays write one stderr notice
  --fields <a,b,...>       Select item-relative fields; preserve envelopes and accept item-key prefixes. api call queries also accept raw output paths
  -o, --output json|table  Choose JSON (default; agent read/session final default to message text) or a supported table layout
  -h, --help               Show help for this command or group
  --version                Print the CLI version

Exit codes: 0 success; 1 operation failure; 2 invalid input; 3 unreachable; 4 unauthorized; 5 incompatible protocol; 6 ambiguous target; 10 waiting observed; 11 agent exited during wait; 124 timeout; 130 interrupted.

Run "opencove <group> --help" for commands in a group.
Start with "opencove api ops --domains" for a compact domain map.
Then run "opencove api ops --prefix node --fields id,cliCommand" to explore one domain.
Run "opencove api ops" only when you need the full catalog.
AI agents: run \`opencove skill\` for a usage guide.
"
`;

exports[`command help snapshots: agent prompt 1`] = `
"Usage: opencove agent prompt --node <target> [--wait [--read]] (<text> | --text <text> | --text-file <path|->) [options]

Send a prompt to an agent window OpenCove launched

Send text to a local Claude Code/Codex window OpenCove launched. Inspect with agent read --screen first: existing drafts may be submitted. Working agents receive steering; --if-busy refuse rejects busy agents. --expect-turn requires the exact active turn. Waiting, unknown/degraded and unsupported sessions refuse.

Agent-terminal prompt rules:
- Launches: describe a task in a sentence; single ASCII words and provider command names are refused; unspaced Chinese/Japanese/Korean text is fine.
- Provider controls: no leading /; Claude also refuses ! # &; Codex, OpenCode, Pi and Gemini also refuse !.
- Exit aliases: no exit, quit, :q, :q!, :wq or :wq!.
- File references: no @ anywhere. Read approved content with opencove fs read --uri file:///workspace/README.md --fields content (replace the absolute URI), then paste the content.
- Codex skills: no $name or linked skill mentions; paste the instructions.
- Leading dashes: Claude Code and Pi launches refuse prompts starting with -.
- Null characters: remove NUL from the prompt.

--wait confirms delivery and waits for its exact turn; --read returns the correlated reply. Check outcome and replyUnavailableReason. Stderr prints IDs and an interruption-only continuation command; final JSON reports results.

--timeout: with --wait, confirmation/waiting default 20s, minimum 250ms, separate 15s send budget; otherwise sending defaults to 15s, maximum 60s.

Never resend after uncertainty, interruption or textComparison: different. Resume agent delivery or agent wait --turn with returned IDs.

Exit codes: 0 submitted/dispatched/turn-ended; 1 refused/unconfirmed; 2 invalid input; 4 authorization; 10 waiting; 11 exited; 124 timeout; 130 interrupted. Details: opencove skill coordinate.

Options:
  --wait                  Confirm delivery and wait for its exact turn to end
  --read                  Include the exact turn reply; requires --wait
  --expect-turn <turnId>  Steer only into this exact active turn; idle or another turn refuses before paste
  --if-busy <if-busy>     Steer the current working turn (default), or refuse
  --node <id>             Select a canvas node by its ID; also accepts @self, @parent, an exact name or a unique ID prefix (6+ characters) (required)
  --text <text>           Prompt text; use this or --text-file
  --text-file <path|->    Read text from a UTF-8 file, or use - for stdin
  --timeout <duration>    Delivery budget, or confirmation + turn wait budget with --wait (e.g. 30s or 2m)
  --space <selector>      Require every node selector, including full IDs, to belong to this space; also accepts @self, an exact name or a unique ID prefix (6+ characters)
  --project <id>          Narrow target lookup to a project; also accepts @self, an exact name or a unique ID prefix (6+ characters)

Table output (-o table): not supported.
Projectable fields: deliveryId, nodeId, sessionId, phase, mode, turnId, textComparison, comparisonProfile, promptId, outcome, endedAt, reply, reply.nodeId, reply.sessionId, reply.turnId, reply.deliveryIds, reply.promptIds, reply.promptId, reply.outcome, reply.message, reply.truncated, reply.messageKind, reply.persisted, reply.memoryOnlyReason, reply.source, replyUnavailableReason

Projection example:
  opencove agent prompt --node node-123 --text "Review the changes" --fields deliveryId
  {"deliveryId":"d"}

Examples:
  opencove agent prompt --node node-123 --wait --read --timeout 2m --text "Review the changes"
  opencove agent prompt --node node-123 --text-file ./prompt.txt --timeout 20s
  opencove agent prompt --node node-123 --expect-turn turn-123 --text "Include refunds"
  cat prompt.txt | opencove agent prompt --node node-123 --text-file -

Global options: see "opencove --help".
"
`;

exports[`command help snapshots: api 1`] = `
"Usage: opencove api <command> [options]

Start with "opencove api ops --domains" for a compact domain map.
Then run "opencove api ops --prefix node --fields id,cliCommand" to explore one domain.
Run "opencove api ops" only when you need the full catalog.

Commands:
  ops     List Control Surface operations with their kind, contract and CLI command
  schema  Show the input JSON Schema for an operation
  call    Invoke a registered operation

Global options: see "opencove --help".
"
`;

exports[`command help snapshots: api call 1`] = `
"Usage: opencove api call <operation-id> [options]

Invoke a registered operation

The input is sent as-is: IDs must be full IDs. Selectors such as @self, names and ID prefixes work only in the dedicated commands; resolve one first, for example: opencove node get --node "@self" --fields id

Options:
  --input <json>         Pass the operation input as JSON
  --input-file <path|->  Read input from a UTF-8 file, or use - for stdin

List output: --limit <n> keeps up to n items per list; 0 means all. Cut object lists add top-level truncated totals; bare arrays keep their shape and write one stderr notice. Cut tables also write one stderr notice.

Table output (-o table): not supported.
Field projection (--fields): queries accept raw output dot paths and item-relative aliases from dedicated commands with declared output fields; validated after the read. Commands do not support projection.

Examples:
  opencove api call project.list
  opencove api call node.create --input-file ./new-note.json

Global options: see "opencove --help".
"
`;

exports[`command help snapshots: api ops --domains 1`] = `
"Usage: opencove api ops [options]

List Control Surface operations with their kind, contract and CLI command

The full JSON catalog is large (about 29 KB). Start with --domains, then narrow with --prefix or --search and --fields. Each operation includes scope (read, interact or admin); inside OpenCove, access comes from the Worker policy (allowed, denied or constrained). Offline or with an older Worker, access is unknown. The allowed flag is omitted for unknown or constrained access.

With --domains, output is an array of summaries with these projectable fields:
  domain: Operation ID prefix before the first dot
  operations: Total operations in the domain after filtering
  schema: Operations with a schema input contract
  commandOperations: Command-kind operations, not dedicated CLI commands

Options:
  --domains                   Count operations, schema contracts and command-kind operations by domain
  --search <text>             Match operation IDs or summaries, ignoring case
  --prefix <domain>           Filter by operation domain, such as node or filesystem
  --kind <query|command>      Filter by operation kind
  --contract <schema|legacy>  Filter by input contract availability

List output: --limit <n> keeps up to n items per list; 0 means all. Cut object lists add top-level truncated totals; bare arrays keep their shape and write one stderr notice. Cut tables also write one stderr notice.

Table output (-o table): supported.
Projectable fields: id, kind, contract, summary, cliCommand, scope, access, allowed, reason, policyVersion, rejectedFields

Projection example:
  opencove api ops --prefix node --fields id,cliCommand
  [{"id":"node.create","cliCommand":"opencove node create"}, ...]

Examples:
  opencove api ops --domains
  opencove api ops --search create
  opencove api ops --prefix node --fields id,cliCommand
  opencove api ops --kind query --contract schema

Global options: see "opencove --help".
"
`;

exports[`command help snapshots: api ops 1`] = `
"Usage: opencove api ops [options]

List Control Surface operations with their kind, contract and CLI command

The full JSON catalog is large (about 29 KB). Start with --domains, then narrow with --prefix or --search and --fields. Each operation includes scope (read, interact or admin); inside OpenCove, access comes from the Worker policy (allowed, denied or constrained). Offline or with an older Worker, access is unknown. The allowed flag is omitted for unknown or constrained access.

With --domains, output is an array of summaries with these projectable fields:
  domain: Operation ID prefix before the first dot
