Global options: see "opencove --help".
"
`;

exports[`command help snapshots: node create agent 1`] = `
"Usage: opencove node create agent [--near <selector> | --root-canvas | --space <id> | <other-target-space-options>] [options]

Create an agent node

Agent-terminal prompt rules:
- Launches: describe a task in a sentence; single ASCII words and provider command names are refused; unspaced Chinese/Japanese/Korean text is fine.
- Provider controls: no leading /; Claude also refuses ! # &; Codex, OpenCode, Pi and Gemini also refuse !.
- Exit aliases: no exit, quit, :q, :q!, :wq or :wq!.
- File references: no @ anywhere. Read approved content with opencove fs read --uri file:///workspace/README.md --fields content (replace the absolute URI), then paste the content.
- Codex skills: no $name or linked skill mentions; paste the instructions.
- Leading dashes: Claude Code and Pi launches refuse prompts starting with -.
- Null characters: remove NUL from the prompt.

Use --prompt or --prompt-file with --wait to collect the initial Claude Code or Codex turn. Add --read for its exact reply. Check outcome: every ended outcome exits 0; unavailable replies return reply: null with replyUnavailableReason. Waiting for the user exits 10, an exited target 11, timeout 124 and interruption 130. Unsupported providers refuse after creation; the node remains.

For several agents, use --prompts-file <path|-> --wait [--read]: a UTF-8 JSON array of 1–10 objects with a nonblank prompt and optional title, at most 1 MiB. All entries are validated before launch. Conflicts: --prompt, --prompt-file, --title, --task, --frame, -q, --fields, --focus and --limit. Target, provider, model and Full Access choices apply to every entry. Add --near @self to place each entry near you; each create finds its own free slot. Creates run in input order; waits run concurrently. Each result or error is retained in an ordered JSON array: {index, title, ok, exitCode, result|error}. index starts at 0; error is the ordinary error envelope. Exit is the first nonzero entry exit; Ctrl-C overrides with 130. Later entries are still attempted after a failure; no launch is retried.

--timeout covers identification and waiting per agent from its creation response (default 20s; minimum 250ms); creation has its own 20s budget. Stderr prints created node/session IDs, then an exact-turn continuation. Errors retain known IDs. Missing telemetry does not prove the agent has stopped. Never repeat creation or resend: inspect the existing session and resume agent wait/read. Ctrl-C stops dispatch and observation, leaves agents running and prints every entry. With a single prompt, -q prints the ID after completion. See opencove skill coordinate for recovery.

For agents creating helper nodes, recommend --near @self to place them near the caller in its current Space (or root). --near is opt-in; combine with --root-canvas to place outside Spaces. Size-only --frame (width/height) is allowed with --near; x/y are refused.

Without explicit x/y, root-canvas placement searches next to you when run inside OpenCove and avoids Spaces. If your node belongs to another project, the search starts at the canvas origin. Use --focus to reveal the node. A --frame with both x and y places the node exactly there, even if it overlaps a Space.

Default target inside OpenCove: your current Space, or the root canvas when you are outside any Space. An explicit --project alone does not select a container; creation also needs --near, --root-canvas or a Space target.

Target container (choose one):
  --root-canvas                                 Create on the project's root canvas, outside every Space
  --space <id>                                  Select a space by its ID; also accepts @self, an exact name or a unique ID prefix (6+ characters)
  --space-name <name> [--project <id>]          Find a named space, optionally within a project
  --worker <id-or-name> --branch <branch>       Find a space by worker and Git branch
  --worker <id-or-name> --path <absolute-path>  Find a space by worker and working directory
  --project <id>                                Select the root-canvas project, or narrow a Space lookup; also accepts @self, an exact name or a unique ID prefix (6+ characters)

Options:
  --prompt <text>             Set the initial agent prompt
  --prompt-file <path|->      Read prompt from a UTF-8 file, or use - for stdin
  --prompts-file <path>       Launch 1–10 prompts from a JSON file or stdin; requires --wait
  --wait                      Wait for the initial prompt turn to end
  --read                      Include the exact initial turn reply; requires --wait
  --timeout <duration>        First-turn identification and wait budget (default 20s; minimum 250ms)
  --provider <id>             Select the agent provider. Accepted IDs: claude-code, codex, opencode, gemini, pi, kimi; omit to use the configured default
  --model <id>                Select the agent model
  --task <nodeId>             Link to an existing task in the same Space and move it to doing
  --full-access <true|false>  Set Full Access for this agent and its restarts; omit to follow Settings
  --title <text>              Set a fixed window name; omit on creation to keep automatic titles
  --frame <json>              Set x, y, width and height as a JSON object. Sizes below a kind's minimum are raised; agent minimum: width 400, height 520; inspect appliedFrame and frameAdjusted for the actual applied size
  --near <selector>           Place near a reference node; recommend --near @self for helper nodes; also accepts @self, @parent, an exact name or a unique ID prefix (6+ characters)
  --focus                     Focus after creation
  -q, --quiet                 Print only the created object identifier followed by a newline

Table output (-o table): not supported.
Projectable fields: id, kind, title, projectId, spaceId, frame, frame.x, frame.y, frame.width, frame.height, status, sessionId, spawnedBy, lineageDepth, lineageStatus, data, data.kind, turnId, outcome, endedAt, reply, reply.nodeId, reply.sessionId, reply.turnId, reply.deliveryIds, reply.promptIds, reply.promptId, reply.outcome, reply.message, reply.truncated, reply.messageKind, reply.persisted, reply.memoryOnlyReason, reply.source, replyUnavailableReason, data.provider, data.prompt, data.model, data.effectiveModel, data.executionDirectory, data.expectedDirectory, data.taskId

Field descriptions:
  spawnedBy      ID of the node that created this node; null for a root or unavailable lineage
  lineageDepth   A node started by the user has lineageDepth 1; each agent-created generation adds one. Null when lineage is unavailable
  lineageStatus  recorded when creation is recorded, root when no parent is recorded, unavailable when lineage cannot be read

Projection example:
  opencove node create agent --space space-123 --fields id,title
  {"revision":1,"projectId":"p","spaceId":"space-123","node":{"id":"n","title":"Agent"}}

Examples:
  opencove node create agent --space space-123 --prompt "Review the test failures" --wait --read --timeout 2m
  opencove node create agent --root-canvas --project project-123 --title "Release review" --pretty

Global options: see "opencove --help".
"
`;

exports[`command help snapshots: node list 1`] = `
"Usage: opencove node list [options]

List canvas nodes

Content by kind: note → data.text; task → data.requirement, data.status, data.priority, data.tags; website → data.url; agent/terminal → status, sessionId. node list returns summaries: use node get for full note text and other content. A requested field absent on a returned kind produces one stderr notice; stdout remains projected JSON.

Lists per-kind summaries: data carries kind plus requirement, status, priority and tags for tasks, or url for websites; data carries only kind for notes, agents and terminals. Read full content, such as note text, with "opencove node get --node <id>".

data.status is task status. Top-level status is session lifecycle (for example running or exited) for agents and terminals, not whether an agent is idle or working. STATE shows task status or session lifecycle; agentState is the separate work-state field. sessionStatus aliases lifecycle status. agentState {state, quality, turnId} reports work for agents and observed agents in terminals; state working means the agent is working. Missing observations are unknown/degraded. context keeps its legacy state field.

Default target inside OpenCove: your current Space. Outside every Space, list all nodes in your project; use --root-canvas to list only root nodes. An explicit --project lists that project.

Target container (choose one):
  --root-canvas                                 List only nodes on the project's root canvas, outside every Space
  --space <id>                                  Select a space by its ID; also accepts @self, an exact name or a unique ID prefix (6+ characters)
  --space-name <name> [--project <id>]          Find a named space, optionally within a project
  --worker <id-or-name> --branch <branch>       Find a space by worker and Git branch
  --worker <id-or-name> --path <absolute-path>  Find a space by worker and working directory
  --project <id>                                Select the project to list, or narrow a Space lookup; also accepts @self, an exact name or a unique ID prefix (6+ characters)

Shows up to 50 results in Worker order. Use --full or --limit 0 for all; --limit <n> overrides the default.

Options:
  --kind <kind>            Filter by note, task, website, agent, terminal, plugin, document, folder, image or role
  --spawned-by <selector>  Filter direct children; --project filters results. Creator names use your project, or the selected Space’s project; also accepts @self, @parent, an exact name or a unique ID prefix (6+ characters)
  --full                   Show all results unless --limit is specified

List output: --limit <n> keeps up to n items per list; 0 means all. Cut object lists add top-level truncated totals; bare arrays keep their shape and write one stderr notice. Cut tables also write one stderr notice.

Table output (-o table): supported.
Projectable fields: id, kind, title, projectId, spaceId, frame, frame.x, frame.y, frame.width, frame.height, status, sessionId, spawnedBy, lineageDepth, lineageStatus, sessionStatus, agentState, agentState.state, agentState.quality, agentState.turnId, taskNodeId, data, data.kind, data.status, data.priority, data.requirement, data.tags, data.url, data.linkedAgentNodeId

Field descriptions:
  spawnedBy      ID of the node that created this node; null for a root or unavailable lineage
  lineageDepth   A node started by the user has lineageDepth 1; each agent-created generation adds one. Null when lineage is unavailable
  lineageStatus  recorded when creation is recorded, root when no parent is recorded, unavailable when lineage cannot be read

Projection example:
  opencove node list --fields id,data.status
  {"projectId":"p","spaceId":null,"nodes":[{"id":"n","data":{"status":"doing"}}]}

Examples:
  opencove node list --space space-123
  opencove node list --space space-123 --fields nodes.id,nodes.title

Global options: see "opencove --help".
"
`;
